# fapibrown: this Mac's warm dark theme, grown from Ghostty's bundled
# Fahrenheit and renamed once its own choices outweighed the original (user).
# Changes from Fahrenheit:
# - slot 7 takes the cream grey #a19981 instead of #ffffce, because secondary
#   text (tool commands and output) and diff code draw in slot 7, and with
#   the bundled value they matched body text; #a19981 sits 1.77:1 below the
#   #d3cda6 foreground. A darker #968d72 (2.05:1) made diff code read too dark
#   once diff rows were tinted; the user lifted it two steps, accepting tool
#   output a little closer to prose.
# - slot 8 takes the cream grey #808067 (5.2:1 on black) instead of #404040,
#   because diff comments and other faint text draw in slot 8 and read darker
#   than dimmed removed lines.
# - slots 1, 2 and 4 take the theme's rust #cb4a05, sage green #97bf7f and
#   light steel blue #86a7cf instead of tan, brown and deep red: every theme
#   keeps real red, yellow (slot 3), green and blue (user standard), for git
#   diffs and the Herdr blocked/working/idle/done states. Each reaches 3:1
#   on the sidebar; the rust red sits close to the gold in hue but
#   far from it in tone (user choice).
# - slot 14 (bright cyan) takes the light steel blue #86a7cf instead of gold
#   #fed04d: Claude Code draws its background-task label ("2 shells") in it,
#   and the gold read off-theme (user). The status line's running-low warning
#   moved to slot 3 #fecf75, so the theme keeps one gold.
# - extended slots 22 and 52 carry Claude Code's diff row tints and 28 and 88
#   its changed-word tints, so no named slot is lent out. Rows: a moss #2a2e19
#   (added) and a rust #3b251e (removed), matched in OKLCH lightness 0.29 and
#   chroma 0.035 with hues on their +/- markers (moss 118 like slot 10, rust
#   40 like slot 9), 1.2:1 off the ground, chosen from four previewed
#   strengths (user); the earlier #202116/#291e19 at 1.05:1 read invisible.
#   Words: the same hues three steps deeper (L 0.41, C 0.065), moss #474f24
#   and rust #683e2f, 1.6:1 off the row (user). The indices keep xterm's
#   meaning (22/28 greens, 52/88 reds), so other tools using them for diffs
#   get the same tints. Claude Code emits ansi256(N) as an indexed color and
#   Ghostty draws this palette for it (the status line's 230 and 173 measure
#   as redefined here).
# - slots 10 (bright green) and 9 (bright red) take moss #acb977 and
#   terracotta #cc734d instead of terracotta and peach: Claude Code draws diff
#   +/- markers and line numbers in them, so they share the hue of the moss and
#   rust row tints, and the added side reads brighter (7.7:1) than the removed
#   one (4.6:1). Code strings (slot 10) turn moss. Extended slots 173
#   (terracotta) and 223 (peach) keep the status-line session name and model.
# - slot 6 (cyan) takes the bundled Fahrenheit tan #cda074 instead of cool
#   grey #979797: Claude Code's code blocks draw built-ins and types in it,
#   and the grey read cool against the warm palette (user).
# - slot 5 takes the deep red #7f0e0f for Herdr's blocked state (user choice;
#   it reads only 1.6:1 on the sidebar, accepted).
# - slot 15 takes the sent-prompt ivory #fff8ea instead of pure white, which
#   stood out too much as status-line figures and bright text (user).
# - extended slot 230 carries the prose cream #d3cda6 for the Claude Code
#   status line: Claude Code dims default-colored status text to grey, so
#   figures meant to match the prose need an explicit slot.
# - slot 1 (red) takes the Fahrenheit orange #fd9f4d (the bundled slot 11):
#   on the warm ground the rust #cb4a05 read muddy as terminal text such as
#   highlighted code, and a brighter #f05806 was passed over (user). The rust
#   stays wherever the user likes it: the Herdr menu dot and Claude Code
#   permission, suggestion and spinner (preset Hex), and status-line warnings
#   through extended slot 166.
# - extended slot 137 keeps the bundled slot 2 brown #9e744d for the status-
#   line directory, which the user preferred to sage and steel blue.
# - slot 12 (inline code in Claude Code) takes terracotta #cc734d (5.0:1). The
#   deep orange #cb4a05 read heavy and muddy as text (3.7:1); it stays for
#   surfaces and accents (Herdr menu dot, permission dialogs, warnings).
# - selection-background takes the deep red #7f0e0f (Herdr blocked),
#   shared with Claude Code and Codex selection: the deep rust #6e2c0c
#   muddied terracotta code text inside it (user). Body text reads 6.6:1.
# - cursor is a warm grey #807c66 block with ivory #fff8ea text instead of
#   cool grey #bbbbbb with white: text under the cursor must stay brighter
#   than the block, because Claude Code draws its voice level as a ▁▂▃█ glyph
#   at the cursor and a cream block with dark text made it fill darker as the
#   voice got louder (user report).
# - background is a warm near-black #1e1c18 instead of pure black: on #000000
#   the message bands and dark surfaces were hard to tell apart (user request),
#   and a lifted ground smears less at night.
# - foreground is a softer cream #d3cda6 instead of #ffffce: with the lifted
#   background, body text sits at 10.6:1 instead of 18.6:1, which the user
#   asked for to cut glare (halation) while keeping the cream tone. Ghostty
#   draws glyphs brighter than the set value (#f2edc4 measured near #fff9d2),
#   so #f2edc4, #e8e2bb, #ddd7b0 and #d8d2ab still glared; it was lowered
#   step by step, the last two steps shared with background lifts from
#   #181612 and #1b1915.
# - faint-opacity 0.42 instead of Ghostty's 0.5: removed diff lines (slot 7
#   drawn faint) read too bright; they now sit at 2.4:1 instead of 2.9:1. It
#   also dims Claude Code's hints, timestamps and placeholder.
# theme-overrides/fahrenheit.toml keeps every downstream target generating from the
# bundled ANSI slots; the lifted background reaches every target.
